\begin{document}
\maketitle

\section{Introduction}
Adamorobe Sign Language (AdaSL, ISO 639-3 ads) is a village sign language of Adamorobe, an Akan village in eastern Ghana. Both deaf and hearing residents use it. It is generally considered an isolate, unrelated to Ghanaian Sign Language, and it is endangered. Deaf children attend a boarding school where Ghanaian Sign Language is the language of instruction.

\section{Primary Educational Resources}
No curricula, primers or school programmes in AdaSL turned up in search results.

\section{Linguistic and Reference Materials}
\begin{itemize}
\item Wikipedia, ``Adamorobe Sign Language'': \url{https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Adamorobe_Sign_Language}
\item Leiden University, Victoria Nyst's work on simultaneous constructions in AdaSL: \url{https://scholarlypublications.universiteitleiden.nl/access/item%3A3589961/view}
\end{itemize}

\section{Religious and Community Resources}
None found.

\section{Digital and Audio Resources}
\begin{itemize}
\item Endangered Languages Project entry: \url{https://endangeredlanguages.com/lang/6909}
\end{itemize}

\section{Curriculum Development Context}
The scholarly literature (Nyst, Kusters) describes the language, but no teaching materials were found. A curriculum would need to be built with the community, taking into account the relationship with Ghanaian Sign Language.
\end{document}
